     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Dear all,<u></u><u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I’m trying to run this\r\nmodel in R (figure below) but hesitate on the approach to adopt:<br></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I can run a Moderated\r\nMultiple Regressions, if I’m correct, with model under R like this:<u></u><u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">model &lt;- ‘ DV1&lt;-lm(DV1~IV*Student+IV*Married+IV*Income)<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"text-indent:36.0pt\">    DV2&lt;-lm(DV2~IV*Age+IV*Sex)’<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Summary (model)<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">But since some of the\r\nvariables are categorical (like the IV, the student and Sex) while others are\r\nsomehow continuous (Income, Age, etc… the 2 DVs have a Poisson distribution\r\nthough), isn’t it more\r\npractical to use the Lavaan package here even though all my variables are\r\nobserved?</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u><u></u></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Thanks a lot for your\r\ninsights!<u></u><u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Jordane<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"> <u></u><u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-no6IfNjme4Q/WiGWLmMrT-I/AAAAAAAAAlM/9HT58HaLAUwSnLwfyZgpI-cS8HZ5PL_SwCLcBGAs/s1600/cdc.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-no6IfNjme4Q/WiGWLmMrT-I/AAAAAAAAAlM/9HT58HaLAUwSnLwfyZgpI-cS8HZ5PL_SwCLcBGAs/s1600/cdc.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426332481000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1_-scqMWqVMVji5NDzy3Uv\"><img src=\"http